No — raise the pool size and it'll just fill up again. The real bug is in `withClient` (db.js:11-15): if `fn(client)` throws, `client.release()` on line 14 never runs, so that connection leaks permanently. The log shows `idle=0` the whole hour while `waiting` climbs steadily (7→28) — classic slow leak, not demand exceeding capacity.

Fix: release in a `finally`:

```js
async function withClient(fn) {
  const client = await pool.connect();
  try {
    return await fn(client);
  } finally {
    client.release();
  }
}
```

