If the connection drops mid-write:

1. The write query (line 17) throws an error when the connection fails.
2. The catch block attempts ROLLBACK (line 21), which will also fail on a dropped connection, throwing a different error.
3. That ROLLBACK error overwrites the original error and gets thrown to the route handler.
4. The finally block releases the broken client back to the pool (it gets discarded).
5. The route handler has no error handling, so the response is never sent and the client gets an abrupt close or hangs until timeout.

The fix is to suppress ROLLBACK errors and always throw the original write error:

```javascript
} catch (err) {
  try {
    await client.query("ROLLBACK");
  } catch (rollbackErr) {
    // Suppress rollback error; connection is already broken
  }
  throw err;
}
```

This ensures the caller sees the actual failure reason, not a secondary ROLLBACK failure.

