turns.text:
Looking at db.js write():

```javascript
try {
  await client.query("BEGIN");
  const res = await client.query(text, params);
  await client.query("COMMIT");
  return res.rows[0];
} catch (err) {
  await client.query("ROLLBACK");
  throw err;
} finally {
  client.release();
}
```

If the connection drops after BEGIN but before COMMIT, the query that detects the drop throws an error into the catch block. The catch block then tries to ROLLBACK on a dead connection, which also fails. That second error masks the first, and you lose the context of what was actually happening.

More critically: the transaction begun on the server side is left hanging. It won't commit, but it stays open until the server times it out (holding locks the whole time). The finally block releases the dead client back to the pool where it'll eventually be culled, but the damage is done.

The fix is to only ROLLBACK if the connection is still alive, and to close the connection explicitly after a drop so it's not reused:

```javascript
async function write(text, params) {
  const client = await pool.connect();
  try {
    await client.query("BEGIN");
    const res = await client.query(text, params);
    await client.query("COMMIT");
    return res.rows[0];
  } catch (err) {
    try {
      await client.query("ROLLBACK");
    } catch (rollbackErr) {
      client.end();
    }
    throw err;
  } finally {
    client.release();
  }
}
```

This way: if ROLLBACK succeeds, release normally. If ROLLBACK fails (connection is dead), end() closes it hard so it won't be reused.

