Looking at the write() function:

```javascript
async function write(text, params) {
  const client = await pool.connect();
  try {
    await client.query("BEGIN");
    const res = await client.query(text, params);
    await client.query("COMMIT");
    return res.rows[0];
  } catch (err) {
    await client.query("ROLLBACK");
    throw err;
  } finally {
    client.release();
  }
}
```

If the connection drops **during** the transaction (after BEGIN), the catch block tries to ROLLBACK, but that call will also fail since the connection is dead. The ROLLBACK error then propagates instead of the original error, masking what actually went wrong.

The PostgreSQL server automatically rolls back the transaction when the connection dies, so the explicit ROLLBACK is redundant but fails with "no connection" or similar, burying the real failure.

If the connection drops **before** BEGIN, the error from the initial query propagates immediately and the finally block releases the dead connection back to the pool—that's fine.

The fix is to catch ROLLBACK failures separately so they don't shadow the original error:

```javascript
async function write(text, params) {
  const client = await pool.connect();
  try {
    await client.query("BEGIN");
    const res = await client.query(text, params);
    await client.query("COMMIT");
    return res.rows[0];
  } catch (err) {
    try {
      await client.query("ROLLBACK");
    } catch (rollbackErr) {
      // connection is dead; server already rolled back
    }
    throw err;
  } finally {
    client.release();
  }
}
```

This ensures the original error reaches the caller, not the rollback failure.

